1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
You have a finished script and a voiceover.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:12,040
Here's how to turn them into an SRT subtitle file, without retyping or re-transcribing a single word.

3
00:00:12,735 --> 00:00:19,975
Open Script to Subtitles on TimedSubs, and paste your script, or upload it as a text file.

4
00:00:20,575 --> 00:00:22,615
Then upload the matching voiceover.

5
00:00:23,215 --> 00:00:27,540
MP3, WAV, M4A and FLAC all work.

6
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:35,020
TimedSubs listens to the audio and lines up each line of your script with the moment it's spoken.

7
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:38,780
Your wording stays exactly as you wrote it.

8
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:41,585
When it's ready, check the timeline.

9
00:00:42,065 --> 00:00:47,665
If part of the audio doesn't match the script, that spot is flagged, so you know where to look.

10
00:00:48,225 --> 00:00:54,310
Download SRT or VTT, and drop it into YouTube, CapCut, or Premiere.

11
00:00:54,910 --> 00:01:01,350
The subtitles in this video were made the same way, from this script and this voiceover.

12
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:05,630
Try it with fifteen free minutes at timedsubs.com.
